# Summit Project - Cursor Rules

## PROJECT VISION AND ARCHITECTURE

### What We Are Building
Summit is an AI-powered autonomous development system that can understand, modify, and improve its own codebase. This is not just another automation tool - we are building a self-evolving AI agent capable of:

- Autonomous code analysis, debugging, and optimization
- Self-directed learning and capability expansion
- Intelligent PR review and code quality enforcement
- Automated testing, linting, and deployment workflows
- Cost-aware API management and resource optimization
- Real-time system monitoring and health management

### Core Technical Principles
- AUTONOMOUS OPERATION: Summit operates independently with minimal human intervention
- SELF-IMPROVEMENT: The system continuously learns and enhances its own capabilities
- QUALITY-FIRST: Every change undergoes rigorous testing, linting, and validation
- COST-AWARE: All operations include cost tracking and budget management
- MODULAR ARCHITECTURE: Clean separation of concerns with well-defined interfaces
- COMPREHENSIVE TESTING: >70% code coverage with automated quality gates

### System Architecture
- MCP (Model Context Protocol) server for AI interactions
- FastAPI web interface for monitoring and control
- Docker containerization for isolated execution environments
- GitHub integration for PR-based workflows
- SQLite database for task and cost tracking
- Comprehensive automation toolkit for code quality

### Development Philosophy
- Write code that other AI agents can understand and modify
- Prioritize automation over manual processes
- Build robust error handling and recovery mechanisms
- Maintain detailed logs and monitoring for system behavior
- Design for scalability and extensibility
- Professional technical communication without emojis or excessive enthusiasm

### Quality Standards
- All code changes require comprehensive tests
- Automated linting and formatting enforcement
- PR-based workflow with quality gates
- Cost tracking and budget controls
- Professional documentation and code comments
- No debugging statements or temporary code in production

This project represents the future of autonomous software development - an AI that can improve itself while maintaining the highest standards of code quality and system reliability.

## MANDATORY Git Operations - USE AGENT GIT WRAPPER ONLY
- YOU MUST USE THE AGENT GIT WRAPPER FOR ALL GIT OPERATIONS
- NEVER use direct git commands (git add, git commit, git push, etc.)
- ALWAYS use the agent_git_api module: `from src.agent_git_api import save_work, create_pr, status, pull`
- Primary function: `save_work("commit message")` - automatically validates, tests, and pushes
- For PRs: `create_pr("commit message", "PR title", "PR description")`
- The wrapper enforces all Summit standards automatically:
  - Single-line commit messages (no line breaks)
  - No emojis in commit messages
  - Runs tests and ensures >70% coverage
  - Runs linting and pre-commit hooks
  - Monitors CI/CD status after push
- Example usage:
  ```python
  from src.agent_git_api import save_work
  # Save all changes
  save_work("Fix PR reviewer tests and improve coverage")
  # Or save specific files
  save_work("Update test configuration", ["tests/test_pr_reviewers.py", "pytest.ini"])
  ```

## MANDATORY PR-Based Workflow
- NEVER push directly to main or master branch
- ALWAYS create Pull Requests for all changes using agent git API
- Use `create_pr()` function which automatically:
  - Creates a new branch if needed
  - Runs full quality checks (tests, linting, coverage)
  - Pushes to feature branch
  - Creates GitHub Pull Request with proper formatting
  - Enables automatic merging when CI/CD passes
- Example usage:
  ```python
  from src.agent_git_api import create_pr
  create_pr(
      commit_message="Fix critical bug in authentication",
      pr_title="Fix authentication bug and improve error handling", 
      pr_description="Detailed description of changes and impact"
  )
  ```

## MANDATORY Code Quality Prevention Rules
- ALWAYS run formatting tools IMMEDIATELY after making any code changes:
  - `python -m black src/ tests/ --line-length 79`
  - `python -m isort src/ tests/ --profile black --line-length 79`
  - `python -m autopep8 --in-place --aggressive --recursive src/ tests/`
- NEVER write lines longer than 79 characters
- ALWAYS use proper import organization:
  - Standard library imports first
  - Third-party imports second (with blank line separation)
  - Local imports last (with blank line separation)
- ALWAYS check line length when writing template strings or long expressions
- BREAK long strings using parentheses and concatenation instead of backslashes
- Example of proper line breaking:
  ```python
  # GOOD
  long_string = (
      "This is a very long string that needs to be broken "
      "across multiple lines to stay under 79 characters"
  )
  
  # BAD
  long_string = "This is a very long string that exceeds the 79 character limit and causes E501 errors"
  ```

## Code Quality Enforcement
- Pre-commit hooks MUST pass before any commit
- Code formatting automatically applied (Black + isort)
- All tests must pass with >70% coverage
- Linting errors must be resolved
- No debugging statements or obvious comments allowed

## Communication Standards
- NO EMOJIS in any documentation, comments, or messages
- NO excessive celebratory language like "MAJOR SUCCESS", "AMAZING", etc.
- NO check mark symbols () or other visual decorations
- Use clear, professional, factual language only
- Report status and results objectively without emotional language
- Focus on technical facts and actionable information

## Documentation Standards
- Keep documentation concise and focused on core capabilities
- Emphasize learning and self-improvement aspects
- Use professional technical writing style
- Avoid marketing language or hype

## Code Comments
- NEVER add obvious/redundant comments like "# Test passed", "# Success", "# End of function"
- Comments should explain WHY, not WHAT the code does
- Avoid stating the obvious - let the code speak for itself
- Only comment when adding genuine value or explaining complex logic

## Testing Rules
- NO AD-HOC TESTING - Never use sleep and curl commands for testing
- FORMAL TEST SUITE ONLY - All testing must be done through pytest or proper test files
- AUTOMATED VALIDATION - Create proper test cases instead of manual command validation
- NEVER SKIP TESTS - Do not add pytest.mark.skip decorators or skip failing tests
- FIX ROOT CAUSE - Address the underlying issue causing test failures instead of avoiding them

## Development Workflow
- Follow the MANDATORY DEVELOPMENT PROCESS from CODING_STANDARDS.md
- Always run tests and coverage before committing
- Use proper Git workflow: analyze → implement → test → lint → commit → push
- Maintain >70% test coverage on all changes

## PROACTIVE QUALITY CHECKLIST
Before making any commit, ALWAYS verify:
1.  Ran automated linting fixes: `python scripts/fix_all_linting.py`
   OR manually run:
   - `python -m black src/ tests/ --line-length 79`
   - `python -m isort src/ tests/ --profile black --line-length 79`  
   - `python -m autopep8 --in-place --aggressive --recursive src/ tests/`
2.  No lines exceed 79 characters
3.  Proper import organization (stdlib, third-party, local with blank lines)
4.  No trailing whitespace
5.  All tests pass
6.  Coverage >70%

## MANDATORY COVERAGE FILE CLEANUP
- ALWAYS cleanup coverage files after any testing operations
- NEVER leave coverage files in the workspace: `coverage.json`, `.coverage`, `coverage.xml`
- Automatically clean these files after running tests:
  ```bash
  rm -f coverage.json .coverage coverage.xml
  ```
- Coverage files are temporary artifacts and should not persist between sessions
- Include coverage cleanup in all testing scripts and workflows

## MANDATORY CONTINUOUS WORK BEHAVIOR
- NEVER STOP WORKING unless explicitly interrupted by the user or there's a push operation
- Continue working on tasks until completion or user intervention
- Do not ask for permission to continue working on related tasks
- Do not pause for confirmation between logical work steps
- Only stop when:
  - User explicitly interrupts or asks to stop
  - A git push operation is required (follow proper PR workflow)
  - A critical error occurs that prevents further progress
- Always maintain forward momentum on task completion
- If one approach fails, immediately try alternative approaches
- Keep working until the user's request is fully satisfied

## MANDATORY TEST FILE PROTECTION
- NEVER DELETE TEST FILES under any circumstances
- Test files provide critical validation and should be preserved
- Pre-commit hooks will block any attempt to delete test files
- If a test needs modification, edit it instead of deleting it
- If a test is truly obsolete, move it to `tests/archived/` for review
- Deleting test files reduces code coverage and quality assurance
- All test files in `tests/` directory are protected from deletion
- This includes files matching patterns: `tests/**/*.py`, `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`

## MANDATORY BUILD STABILITY RESPONSIBILITY
- THE BUILD WORKS WHEN PULLED FROM MAIN - any breaks are caused by agent changes
- If tests pass on main branch but fail after agent modifications, agent MUST fix the issues
- NEVER ignore or skip failing tests that worked before agent changes
- NEVER blame "existing issues" for failures introduced by agent modifications  
- Agent is responsible for maintaining full system functionality
- If agent changes break Docker builds, CI/CD, or any existing functionality - agent must fix it
- No partial fixes or "good enough" solutions when agent breaks working functionality
- Main branch represents the working baseline - agent changes must maintain or improve that standard